A destroy that does not revoke below the pool's own node
takes the objects while capabilities still name them.
The revoke that met the node clears those only once the pool is gone and they came up to it with the node,
so in between they name dead objects.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: dangling object capability

diff --git a/kernel/pool.c b/kernel/pool.c
--- a/kernel/pool.c
+++ b/kernel/pool.c
@@ -78,12 +78,6 @@ bool pool_destroy(struct pool *pool, bool preempt)
      * so revoking below it leaves none.
      * Its progress is in the tree, and a new capability made meanwhile goes the same way.
      */
-    while (cap_revoke_step(&pool->node)) {
-        LOOP_PAID(pool_destroy, node, "a capability to the pool or to one of its objects");
-        if (cap_stop_here(preempt)) {
-            return false;
-        }
-    }
 
     /*
      * The objects go newest first, and the used mark follows,
